Claim 20 — Telegram goal UX
===========================

Goal: inspect the Telegram goal user-experience for inline-button
approve / request-changes / reject of plans, reply-to-plan-message
edits, reply-to-blocked-message answers, context preservation across
replies, and operator-feedback-creates-follow-up-steps. Classify each
sub-feature as verified / partial / unverified / manual-required based
on whether the code path is statically present AND whether an
automated test exists.

Method: static inspection (Grep + Read) only. No live Telegram message
was sent.

Sub-feature inventory and per-sub-feature status
------------------------------------------------

A) Inline-button "Approve / Plan Detail / Request changes / Reject" on
   the plan message.

   Buttons attached to plan message:
     src/telegram/goal-sending.ts:118-123

         { text: "❤️ Approve",       callback_data: `ga:${runIdPrefix}:${revision}` },
         { text: "🔍 Plan Detail",   callback_data: `gD:${runIdPrefix}:${revision}` },
         { text: "✏️ Request changes", callback_data: `ge:${runIdPrefix}:${revision}` },
         { text: "👎 Reject",        callback_data: `gr:${runIdPrefix}:${revision}` },

   Callback-query handler routes the four prefixes:
     src/telegram/goal-commands.ts:1479-1483

         bot.on("callback_query:data", async (ctx, next) => {
           const data = ctx.callbackQuery.data;
           const planMatch = /^(ga|gD|gr|ge):([a-f0-9-]+):(\d+)$/.exec(data);

   Action branches:
     src/telegram/goal-commands.ts:1499-1517 — emoji reaction
       (❤ approve, 👀 detail, 👎 reject, ✍ edit) on the plan message.
     :1520-1562 — `ge` action sends a ForceReply prompt and persists
       the edit-prompt message so the router can map the user's reply
       back to GOAL_EDIT for run-id resolution.
     :1578-1599 — `ga` action calls startGoalResume; `gD` action calls
       sendGoalDetailResponse; `gr` action handles rejection.

   Automated tests (callback handler invoked via test harness):
     src/telegram/goal-commands.test.ts:4583 — `ga:abcdef12:1` →
       expects START_PREFACE reply.
     src/telegram/goal-commands.test.ts:4593 — `ga:deadbeef:1` →
       expects "Run not found: deadbeef".
     src/telegram/goal-commands.test.ts:4610 — `gr:abcdef12:1` →
       expects "Plan rejected" string in reply.
     src/telegram/goal-commands.test.ts:4815 — `gD:abcdef12:1` →
       exercises detail callback.
     src/telegram/goal-commands.test.ts:4255 — `ge:abcdef12:1` —
       edit-prompt callback test.

   Status (this sub-feature): partial.
   Reason: the handler is statically present and exercised by unit
   tests through a mocked callback harness, but no live Telegram
   round-trip with a real Bot API was run in this audit. A live
   inline-button click can only be verified manually.

B) Reply-to-plan-message → GOAL_EDIT.

   Router:
     src/telegram/goal-router.ts:207-213

         // GOAL_EDIT: reply to the latest plan message.
         const latestMatch = scopedRuns.find(
           (run) => run.telegramPlanMessage?.messageId === replyToMessageId,
         );
         if (latestMatch) {
           return { kind: "GOAL_EDIT", runId: latestMatch.runId };
         }

   Also routes replies to the ForceReply edit-prompt sent by the
   "Request changes" button:
     src/telegram/goal-router.ts:215-223
       (matches against `run.telegramEditPromptMessages`)

   Older-revision disambiguation:
     src/telegram/goal-router.ts:256-262
       (replies to an older plan revision route to DISAMBIGUATE rather
        than silently editing the wrong revision).

   Automated test:
     src/telegram/goal-router.test.ts:76 — "routes reply to latest
       plan message to GOAL_EDIT".
     src/telegram/goal-router.test.ts:94 — "routes reply to
       edit-prompt message to GOAL_EDIT".
     src/telegram/goal-router.test.ts:152 — wrong-chat scope test.

   Status (this sub-feature): partial.
   Reason: routing logic is statically present and has direct unit
   tests. A live Telegram reply UX is not exercised here, so the
   end-to-end UX is manual-required for full confirmation.

C) Reply-to-blocked-message → GOAL_ANSWER.

   Router:
     src/telegram/goal-router.ts:245-254

         // GOAL_ANSWER: reply to a question/clarification message from a blocked run.
         const questionMatch = findRunByQuestionMessageId(
           scopedRuns,
           chatId,
           threadId,
           replyToMessageId,
         );
         if (questionMatch) {
           return { kind: "GOAL_ANSWER", runId: questionMatch.runId };
         }

   Lookup function:
     src/telegram/goal-router.ts:158-167 — matches against
       `run.telegramQuestionMessages` and isBlockedRun.

   Automated test:
     src/telegram/goal-router.test.ts:416 — "routes reply to question
       message to GOAL_ANSWER".
     src/telegram/goal-router.test.ts:469 — "does not route reply to
       question message if run is no longer blocked".

   Status (this sub-feature): partial.
   Reason: routing logic is present and unit-tested; the live Telegram
   side of the round-trip (user reads bot question, replies with
   answer, executor unblocks) requires a live bot to verify
   end-to-end.

D) Context preservation across replies.

   Scoping is by (chatId, threadId): only runs that match the current
   chat + topic thread are considered for routing.
     src/telegram/goal-router.ts:190-194

         const scopedRuns = filterRunsForChatThread({
           runs: input.runs,
           chatId,
           threadId,
         });

   Per-run Telegram message arrays persisted in the run record (used
   by the router):
     src/goal/types.ts (Run record fields)
       telegramPlanMessage         — latest plan message id (+ history)
       telegramEditPromptMessages  — ForceReply edit prompts
       telegramDoneMessage         — done message id
       telegramFeedbackPromptMessages — ForceReply feedback prompts
       telegramQuestionMessages    — blocked-run question messages

   These are the lookup tables that let the router map an inbound
   reply back to the right run + intent.

   Automated test:
     src/telegram/goal-router.test.ts — multiple test cases covering
       wrong-chat and wrong-thread negatives (e.g. line 152: "does
       not match edit-prompt message from wrong chatId").

   Status (this sub-feature): partial.
   Reason: the data model and the matching function are statically
   present and unit-tested for chat/thread scoping. The end-to-end
   context preservation across a live Telegram thread (reactions,
   typing indicators, message ordering) is manual-required.

E) Operator feedback creates follow-up steps (the "Incorporate
   Feedback" flow).

   Inline button on the done message:
     src/telegram/goal-formatting.ts:328

         [{ text: "🔄 Incorporate Feedback", callback_data: `gIF:${runIdPrefix}` }]

   Callback handler `gIF:` is registered in goal-commands.ts; the
   handler sends a ForceReply asking the operator to describe what
   they noticed, then persists the prompt message via
   persistFeedbackPromptMessage (src/telegram/goal-sending.ts:298).

   Routing of the reply back to GOAL_FEEDBACK:
     src/telegram/goal-router.ts:225-243
       — reply to done message → GOAL_FEEDBACK
       — reply to feedback-prompt message → GOAL_FEEDBACK

   Plan revision logic (the "create follow-up steps" part):
     src/goal/feedback.ts:46-56

         export function buildFeedbackRevisionInstructions(feedbackText: string): string {
           return [
             "Manual test feedback was provided after execution.",
             "Revise the plan to add only the additional fix/improvement steps needed.",
             "Keep previously completed steps represented in the revised plan (same IDs when possible).",
             "Do not remove already completed work.",
             "",
             `Feedback: ${feedbackText.trim()}`,
           ].join("\n");
         }

     src/goal/feedback.ts:62-101 — mergeRevisedPlanWithDoneSteps:
       preserves all done steps from the original plan and merges in
       the newly-planned follow-up steps.

   Automated test:
     src/goal/feedback.test.ts — covers buildFeedbackRevisionInstructions
       and mergeRevisedPlanWithDoneSteps (preserve-done-steps merge,
       dangling dep stripping, etc.).
     src/telegram/goal-router.test.ts:113 — "routes reply to
       feedback-prompt message to GOAL_FEEDBACK".
     src/telegram/goal-router.test.ts:133 — "routes reply to done
       message to GOAL_FEEDBACK".
     src/telegram/goal-commands.test.ts:1019/1034 — keyboard
       construction includes "🔄 Incorporate Feedback".

   Status (this sub-feature): partial.
   Reason: revision logic has direct unit tests; routing has direct
   unit tests; Telegram inline button is present. Full end-to-end
   (button click → ForceReply → operator types feedback → revised
   plan appears in the same chat) is manual-required.

Skipped checks
--------------

  * No live Telegram message exchange (out of scope for this read-only
    audit; would require gateway restart and a bot token).
  * The Bot API surface itself (grammy `bot.api.sendMessage`,
    `setMessageReaction`, `answerCallbackQuery`) was not mocked at the
    network level; the unit tests stub the Bot/api and verify the
    arguments and routing.

Aggregate status reasoning
--------------------------

Per the audit's classification rule ("Telegram UX with inline buttons /
reply context that has no automated test must be classified
manual-required or partial, never verified"), even sub-features with
automated unit tests for routing/handler logic cannot be classified
verified — the live UI/UX side requires a Telegram round-trip. Every
sub-feature here has at least handler/routing test coverage, but none
have an automated live Telegram exchange, so the overall claim sits at
partial.

Safe README wording (drawn from code, not aspirational)
-------------------------------------------------------

  * "Plan messages carry inline buttons for Approve, Plan Detail,
    Request changes, and Reject."
  * "Reply to the plan message to revise it in natural language;
    reply to a blocked question to unblock the run; reply to the
    done message to suggest follow-up work via Incorporate
    Feedback."
  * "Routing is scoped by chat and topic thread so replies are
    matched against the right run."

Unsafe wording to avoid
-----------------------

  * "Verified end-to-end via Telegram in this release audit" — no
    live Telegram message was sent.
  * "Reply context preservation guaranteed" — context preservation is
    a chat/thread scoping heuristic on a per-run lookup table; live
    Telegram behavior is manual-required.
  * "One click and the bot rewrites your plan" — Request changes is a
    ForceReply that prompts the operator to describe their change;
    the bot does not edit the plan unilaterally.
  * "Incorporate Feedback fixes the bug automatically" — it queues a
    plan revision based on the operator's typed feedback; the worker
    still has to do the work.

STATUS: partial
REASON: every sub-feature (inline buttons, plan-reply edit, blocked-
reply answer, chat/thread scoping, Incorporate Feedback follow-up
steps) has its code path statically present and unit-tested at the
router / handler / revision level. No live Telegram round-trip was
performed in this audit, so the live UX cannot be classified verified.
